    },
    {
      "id": "f4923ce5f4e5341f85e8b648baa61d99cf05f156",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 2153260694,
          "type": null,
          "body": "Indeed, this is a manifestation of C not being a format specification language. It leaves too many things to be defined by the (compiler/library) implementation. Therefore syntactic sugar, like `packed` is needed to try to convince the compiler to do certain things with the layout of data. But there's more to the story, e.g. endianness issues, unaligned accesses, etc. <br/> The early Wireshark developers has painstakingly worked on creating the methods needed to be able to handle packet data safely, through the TVB in particular.",
          "author": {
            "id": 2398962,
            "username": "JaapKeuter",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Jaap Keuter",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/722b0c520542a23cb8f8ea7070a45c88f9e9214dc700ac151e419e9649e0304e?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/JaapKeuter"
          },
          "created_at": "2024-10-10T18:28:04.271Z",
          "updated_at": "2024-10-10T18:28:04.271Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 332721630,
